421234 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421234 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186421176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421234 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eea0f599-a5c9-5a71-bef4-9b89d6f5c991" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421235 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421235 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162548" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421235 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421235 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,x+1,\\,-y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=x-4y+1$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 4.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=3\\sqrt{2}.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421235 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f0f5aff-a329-55d0-a382-ebd7a7992774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421235 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x-4y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,4,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x-4y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,x+1,\\,-y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨x-4y+1,\\,x+1,\\,-y⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}(x-4y+1-4(x+1)+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}(-3x-3y-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-x-y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-x-y-1)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-x-y-1)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-x-y-1)\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}}(-x-y-1)\\cdot 3\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-3{∬}_{D}(x+y+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+xy+x]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2y+4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3[{y}^{2}+4y]_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =-96\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421236 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421236 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161202" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421236 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421236 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨\\frac{z}{2},\\,x,\\,z-y⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,\\,0,\\,-3⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421236 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f3b4139-4781-5e1e-8511-61fe405f4cab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421236 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨\\frac{z}{2},\\,x,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨4,\\,0,\\,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨\\frac{z}{2},\\,x,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot ⟨4,\\,0,\\,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2z+0-3(z-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y-z}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{3y-z}{5}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{S}3y-z\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421237 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421237 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162157" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421237 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421237 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z+x,\\,x+y,\\,2x+6⟩$ and a finite region $S$ of the plane $z=-x+2y$ such that $\\text{Area}(S)=2\\sqrt{3}.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421237 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "49459c91-bfac-5582-aa3d-bc1bfea60a7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421237 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=-x+2y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=-x+2y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z+x,\\,x+y,\\,2x+6⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2y,\\,x+y,\\,2x+6⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2y-2(x+y)+2x+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421238 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421238 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161211" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421238 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421238 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{x}^{2},\\,2xz,\\,x+y⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{2xy+2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}z}{\\boxed{}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421238 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5213d5bf-5843-5c8c-8790-014b534c60aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421238 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩}{\\sqrt{(-3z)^{2}+(x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩}{\\sqrt{9{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨{x}^{2},\\,2xz,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨{x}^{2},\\,2xz,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3{x}^{2}z+2{x}^{2}z+2y(x+y)}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy+2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{2xy+2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421239 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421239 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161197" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421239 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421239 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨2y,\\,2x,\\,2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}$ and $0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{4}.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421239 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6480541e-888b-58cb-a4eb-1a531ae87114" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421239 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is a  continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=1$ for  $0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}$ and $0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{4},$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨2y,\\,2x,\\,2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2y,\\,2x,\\,4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-4)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/4}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(-4)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\cdot [y]_{0}^{1/4}\\cdot [x]_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421240 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421240 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161208" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421240 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421240 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨2z,\\,xy,\\,2⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨xy,\\,-z,\\,y⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{xyz+2y}{\\boxed{}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421240 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75809447-c869-5206-aa76-8147e22ce3d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421240 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨xy,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{(xy)^{2}+(-z)^{2}+(y)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨xy,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨xy,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨2z,\\,xy,\\,2⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨xy,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2z,\\,xy,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨xy,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xyz-xyz+2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xyz+2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{xyz+2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421241 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421241 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161243" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421241 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421241 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,2y,\\,2z-x-y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=x-\\frac{y}{2}+3$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\frac{3}{2}.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421241 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa928e5a-70af-5b00-a980-606761f10bda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421241 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x-\\frac{y}{2}+3$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=3,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-3.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x-\\frac{y}{2}+3$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,2y,\\,2z-x-y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,2y,\\,(2x-y+6)-x-y⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,2y,\\,x-2y+6⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x-2y-2(x-2y+6)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2y-12).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{3}(2y-12)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}(2y-12)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}(2y-12)\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{3}}(2y-12)\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{3}}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-6)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(y-6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(y-6)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[x]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-6y]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-24\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421242 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421242 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161206" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421242 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421242 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨y,\\,-z,\\,x⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨2z,\\,y,\\,3{x}^{2}⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{\\boxed{}}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421242 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b743d271-bfde-5d80-a2b4-b7cbdaa6f1f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421242 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2z,\\,y,\\,3{x}^{2}⟩}{\\sqrt{(2z)^{2}+(y)^{2}+{(3{x}^{2})}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2z,\\,y,\\,3{x}^{2}⟩}{\\sqrt{4{z}^{2}+{y}^{2}+9{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2z,\\,y,\\,3{x}^{2}⟩}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,-z,\\,x⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨2z,\\,y,\\,3{x}^{2}⟩}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨y,\\,-z,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨2z,\\,y,\\,3{x}^{2}⟩}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2yz-yz+3{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}+yz}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{3{x}^{3}+yz}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421243 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421243 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161204" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421243 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421243 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,xy,\\,{y}^{2}-1⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨{y}^{2},\\,2x,\\,-z⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{2{x}^{2}y+z}{\\boxed{}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421243 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7df86f0-3809-56f0-adcd-fb286616dfa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421243 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨{y}^{2},\\,2x,\\,-z⟩}{\\sqrt{{({y}^{2})}^{2}+(2x)^{2}+(-z)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨{y}^{2},\\,2x,\\,-z⟩}{\\sqrt{{y}^{4}+4{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨{y}^{2},\\,2x,\\,-z⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,xy,\\,{y}^{2}-1⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨{y}^{2},\\,2x,\\,-z⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,xy,\\,{y}^{2}-1⟩\\cdot ⟨{y}^{2},\\,2x,\\,-z⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}z+2{x}^{2}y-z({y}^{2}-1)}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}y+z}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}y+z}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421244 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421244 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161186" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421244 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421244 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y-x,\\,x+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421244 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4c6d18b-d618-53cd-9fce-a599b57a0c5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421244 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=2$ for  $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y-x,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,y-x,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =x+y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
